{
  "_about": [
    "Golden cases for the delete lane frame and for the rule that decides whether a delete may withdraw a message.",
    "Normative: docs/sealed-messages.md §Deletes.",
    "The deleter is never in the payload: it is the MLS sender, read from the decrypted credential. Parsing is strict JSON, as for reactions; an unknown extra key is ignored.",
    "`encode` and `parse` cases pin the wire. `honor` cases pin the author check: `sender` is the delete's MLS sender; `record` is the sender this device recorded when it opened the target, or null when it holds none; `author` is the author the device otherwise knows for the row, or \"\" when it knows none.",
    "With a `record`, honor exactly when `sender` equals it, whatever `author` says. With no record, honor exactly when `sender` equals `author`. With neither, refuse.",
    "`pending` cases pin how waiting deletes are kept. Each is a list of steps: `marker` records a delete from `sender` for `messageId`; `target` asks whether the message is withdrawn now (`expect`), given its `record` and `author`. A true answer is followed by forgetting that id.",
    "Deletes are kept per (message id, sender), so no sender can displace or block another. When the target is attributable (a record, or else a non-empty author), every kept sender is judged; any pass withdraws the message, and every sender that fails is dropped. When it is not, every delete waits.",
    "The case's `pending` is what is left afterward: message id to sorted senders.",
    "To replay: run each case through your own implementation of the format and compare the result with the case's expectation, field by field."
  ],
